  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 113 PM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nCurrent H5 and water vapor analysis shows the center of the\nmid/upper low over SE Nebraska, moving to the northeast.\nMeanwhile, the surface cyclone is currently centered over\nsouthern Iowa, with the attendant cold front draped north to \nsouth across Missouri. Showers continue across portions of the\nCWA, primarily toward Mid-Missouri and across NW Missouri into\neastern Kansas. Instability remains quite paltry, which has\nlimited thunderstorm development, and the most recent SPC\nconvective outlook has removed almost all of the CWA from the \nmarginal risk (except for NE Missouri). Winds will eventually\nturn west northwesterly as the surface low continues to move to\nthe northeast, with gusts up to 25 mph through the evening and\ntonight. Patchy drizzle will be possible tonight with lingering\nlow level saturation and subtle forcing for ascent. Colder air \nwill filter into the region overnight, with morning lows falling\ninto the mid to upper 40s for most locations. Cloud cover \nshould linger into tomorrow, with skies finally beginning to \nclear from southwest to northeast by tomorrow afternoon \n(although quite a bit of cloud cover should remain across \nnorthern and NE Missouri), with afternoon highs in the mid to \nupper 50s and breezy westerly winds.\n\nNear normal temperatures are likely to continue through the end\nof the work week, with afternoon highs in the mid to upper 50s\nand overnight lows around 40 degrees. Skies should finally\nbecome clear CWA wide by Wednesday night into Thursday\nafternoon, but another trough is progged to move across the \ncentral US Thursday afternoon into Thursday night, which will\nbring increased cloud cover and slight chance PoPs (20%). High\npressure is anticipated to build overhead Friday into Friday\nnight, which will help lower temperatures Friday night into \nSaturday morning into the mid to upper 30s for many locations, \nwith some patchy frost possible. A warming trend is likely this\nweekend into early next week, with high temperatures returning\nto the mid to upper 60s by Monday with dry conditions likely\npersisting.",
